| Detection Metadata                                                                    |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Detection Name    : LSASS Access Simulation — Credential Dumping Indicator            |
| Detection ID      : DET-003                                                           |
| MITRE Tactic      : Credential Access                                                 |
| MITRE Technique   : T1003.001 — OS Credential Dumping: LSASS Memory                  |
| Severity          : Critical                                                          |
| Data Sources      : Sysmon Event 10 (ProcessAccess), WinEvent 4656                   |
| Splunk Index      : index=endpoint                                                    |

================================================================================
PRIMARY QUERY — LSASS Process Access Detection
================================================================================

index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational"
EventID=10
TargetImage="*\\lsass.exe"
NOT SourceImage IN (
    "*\\MsMpEng.exe",
    "*\\svchost.exe",
    "*\\csrss.exe",
    "*\\wininit.exe",
    "*\\services.exe",
    "*\\lsass.exe",
    "*\\System",
    "*\\SecurityHealthService.exe"
)
| eval detection="LSASS Process Access — Credential Dumping Indicator"
| eval mitre="T1003.001"
| eval severity="Critical"
| eval risk_note=case(
    like(GrantedAccess,"0x1010"), "READ_CONTROL + VM_READ — classic mimikatz mask",
    like(GrantedAccess,"0x1410"), "READ_CONTROL + VM_READ + QUERY_INFO — high risk",
    like(GrantedAccess,"0x1fffff"), "FULL ACCESS — extreme risk",
    1=1, concat("Access mask: ", GrantedAccess, " — review CallTrace")
  )
| table _time, ComputerName, User, SourceImage, TargetImage, GrantedAccess, CallTrace, risk_note
| sort -_time

================================================================================
ANALYST TRIAGE STEPS
================================================================================
1. Note SourceImage — what process is accessing LSASS?
2. Check GrantedAccess mask against known-bad masks (0x1010, 0x1410, 0x143A)
3. Review CallTrace for known-bad DLL paths (temp dirs, unsigned DLLs)
4. Check if SourceImage is a security product (AV, EDR) — allowlist if confirmed
5. If unknown process: check process creation event (Event 1) for its CommandLine
6. If confirmed malicious: ISOLATE HOST immediately

================================================================================
KNOWN FALSE POSITIVES
================================================================================
- Antivirus and EDR agents that legitimately inspect LSASS
- Windows Defender / MsMpEng.exe (already excluded above)
- Some backup and performance monitoring tools
- LSASS debugging (rare but legitimate in dev environments)
